    <section class="intro"><div class="eyebrow">Interactive research companion <span>Chile · 2005–2024</span></div><h1>Private School Competition<br> and Student Achievement in Chile</h1><p class="lede">The evidence changes with the grade. Explore the estimates, the choices behind them, and the limits of what they tell us.</p><div class="byline">Alex Tabarrok <span>George Mason University</span><span class="snapshot">Results snapshot · 16 September 2026</span></div></section>
    <section id="explorer" aria-labelledby="explorer-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Explore the evidence</span><h2 id="explorer-title">One paper. Different views of the evidence.</h2></div><span class="provenance">Reported manuscript results</span></div>
      <p class="explorer-intro">The paper uses several models to examine the relationship between private school competition and student achievement. Click the tabs below to compare panel estimates, results by grade, cohort value added, and cumulative exposure, or explore different definitions of the schooling market. Within each tab, choose a specification or click a chart row to see its estimate and explanation.</p>
      <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Analysis"><button role="tab" id="tab-panel" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="analysis-panel" data-view="panel">Panel estimates</button><button role="tab" id="tab-gradient" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="analysis-panel" tabindex="-1" data-view="gradient">By grade</button><button role="tab" id="tab-cohort" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="analysis-panel" tabindex="-1" data-view="cohort">Cohort value added</button><button role="tab" id="tab-exposure" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="analysis-panel" tabindex="-1" data-view="exposure">Cumulative exposure</button><button role="tab" id="tab-market" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="analysis-panel" tabindex="-1" data-view="market">Market definition</button></div>
      <div class="workspace" id="analysis-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-panel">
        <aside class="context"><span class="eyebrow" id="design-tag">Changes across markets and time</span><h3 id="design-title">What the panel controls for</h3><p id="design-description">The main specification uses commune fixed effects. Compare it with grade and subject splits, and commune × grade fixed effects.</p><div class="cohort-path" id="cohort-path" hidden><span>Grade 4<small>Baseline achievement</small></span><span class="path-line" aria-hidden="true">→</span><span>Grade 10<small>Outcome six years later</small></span></div><label for="spec-select">Choose a specification</label><select id="spec-select"><option>Panel: commune fixed effects</option></select><p class="context-note" id="design-note">The main pooled estimate also uses persistent cross-grade differences. The stricter specification removes those differences.</p></aside>
        <div class="results"><div class="chart-heading"><div><h3 id="chart-title">Panel estimates</h3><p>Private-share coefficient · SIMCE points</p></div><span class="legend"><i></i> Estimate ± 1 standard error</span></div><div id="chart" class="chart" aria-label="Coefficient comparison"><div class="initial-result"><strong>10.52</strong><span>Main panel estimate · standard error 2.9</span></div></div><p class="chart-note">Lines show one standard error, not a 95% confidence interval. Select a row to inspect its result.</p><div class="result-detail" aria-live="polite"><div><span class="eyebrow" id="result-label">Panel: commune fixed effects</span><div class="estimate"><strong id="estimate">10.52</strong><span>coefficient <span id="significance">p &lt; 0.01</span></span></div></div><dl><div><dt>Standard error</dt><dd id="se">2.9</dd></div><div><dt>Observations</dt><dd id="n">19,221</dd></div><div><dt>Clusters</dt><dd id="clusters">336</dd></div></dl></div><p class="result-explanation" id="result-explanation">Main panel specification, with commune and grade × subject × year fixed effects. Weighted by tested students; errors clustered by commune.</p><div class="source-line"><a id="source-link" href="/research/chile/sources/tab_panel_full.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View source table ↗</a><button id="download-result" class="text-button">Download this result ↓</button></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway"><span>What to take from this</span><p id="takeaway">The main pooled estimate is 10.52. With commune × grade fixed effects it falls to 5.42. The grade-specific estimates do not depend on that fixed-effect choice.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="magnitude-section" aria-labelledby="magnitude-title"><div class="magnitude-intro"><span class="eyebrow">Put the estimate in context</span><h2 id="magnitude-title">What does a change in private share imply?</h2><p>Translate the selected coefficient into a change in the population’s mean score.</p><label for="share-change">Increase in private share <output id="share-output" for="share-change">10 percentage points</output></label><input type="range" id="share-change" min="1" max="30" value="10"><div class="range-labels"><span>1 pp</span><span>30 pp</span></div></div><div class="magnitude-results" aria-live="polite"><div><span>Commune mean</span><strong id="points">+1.052</strong><span>SIMCE points</span></div><div><span>In individual test-score units</span><strong id="sd">+0.0210</strong><span>standard deviations</span></div><p id="formula">10.52 × 0.10 = 1.052 points. The paper uses an individual test-score SD of 50.</p><details><summary>How does this relate to the per-student benchmark?</summary><p id="per-student">The implied per-student benchmark is 0.210 SD if gains accrue to students moving sectors and competition and peer effects on non-movers are small. It is not the population-mean effect shown above.</p></details></div></section>
    <section id="paper-agent" class="agent-section" aria-labelledby="agent-title">
      <div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Run the evidence</span><h2 id="agent-title">Ask the paper. Inspect the calculation.</h2></div><span class="provenance">Paper2Agent · Pilot</span></div>
      <p>The agent can run the main cohort model and its commune fixed-effects alternative on the paper’s data. Each run returns uncertainty, sample counts, and a downloadable record. Other specifications above remain reported manuscript results.</p>
      <div class="agent-grid">
        <div class="agent-chat">
          <h3>A conversation with the evidence</h3><p id="agent-status" role="status">Checking availability…</p>
          <form id="agent-chat-form"><label for="agent-question">Your question</label><textarea id="agent-question" maxlength="2000" rows="3" placeholder="Compare the two models. What does a 10-point increase in private share imply?" required></textarea>
          <label for="agent-access">Pilot access code</label><input id="agent-access" type="password" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Provided by the author"><p class="context-note">Questions and recent conversation are sent to OpenAI to generate an answer. Do not enter confidential information.</p><button id="agent-send" class="primary-link" type="submit" disabled>Ask the paper</button></form>
          <div id="agent-conversation" aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="agent-run"><h3>Run a model directly</h3><p>No AI conversation is needed for these calculations.</p>
          <form id="agent-run-form"><label for="agent-model">Cohort specification</label><select id="agent-model"><option value="baseline">Main cohort model</option><option value="commune">With commune fixed effects</option></select><label for="agent-pp">Change in private share (percentage points)</label><input id="agent-pp" type="number" min="-100" max="100" step="any" value="10" required><button class="primary-link" type="submit">Run the regression</button></form>
          <div id="agent-result" aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="context-note">Built with Paper2Agent’s conversion and verification workflow. Python calculations were checked against Stata. The agent supports two cohort specifications; it does not identify individual student effects or establish causality by itself. <a href="https://github.com/jmiao24/Paper2Agent">About Paper2Agent ↗</a></p>
    </section>

    <section id="questions" class="questions-section"><div><span class="eyebrow">Read the evidence closely</span><h2>Questions worth asking</h2><p>Answers drawn from the manuscript.<br>This first edition uses curated explanations.</p></div><div class="answers">
      <details><summary>What does “private share” measure?</summary><p>In the baseline, private share refers to subsidized-private enrollment in the public-plus-subsidized-private school market. Fully private schools and communes dominated by fully private schooling are excluded. Outcomes describe students attending schools in the commune, rather than necessarily living there. The analysis uses reported market definitions and tested-student weights.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does this overturn Hsieh and Urquiola’s finding?</summary><p>It refines the comparison by grade. The grade-4 long-difference estimate is 4.16 (SE 4.46), small and imprecise. Grade-10 long differences and cohort value added are stronger. Hsieh and Urquiola’s test-score evidence concerned grade 4.</p><a href="#explorer" data-jump="gradient">Compare the grades ↑</a></details>
      <details><summary>What changes when commune fixed effects are added?</summary><p>In the cohort design, the estimate changes from 14.05 (SE 2.471) to 0.40 (SE 4.39). Commune fixed effects remove persistent differences across communes. Within-commune private share varies much less across cohorts: its standard deviation is 4.1 percentage points, compared with 22.7 before removing those differences. The result is near zero and imprecise; the baseline association is not established by this stricter comparison.</p><a href="#explorer" data-jump="cohort" data-spec="va-commune">Inspect the commune-FE result ↑</a></details>
      <details><summary>Are the same students followed for six years?</summary><p>No individual student panel is used. The design matches a commune’s grade-4 mean in year t to its grade-10 mean in year t + 6, separately by subject. Migration, cross-commune attendance, grade progression, and changing test participation can alter the observed cohort. The paper examines cohort fidelity and alternative market definitions.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does the paper establish a causal effect?</summary><p>The designs establish conditional relationships under different assumptions. The cohort analysis controls for prior achievement, but changes after grade 4 that affect both private enrollment and achievement can still confound the estimate. Sorting within a fixed tested population cannot change its mean; changes in who belongs to that population can. The robustness results help assess these explanations without eliminating all of them.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do longer exposure windows identify when the gains occur?</summary><p>Exposure estimates are consistent with cumulative schooling effects, but the measures correlate above 0.94. The comparisons do not sharply identify the timing of gains. Enrollment is observed during the testing gap, so exposure can be constructed even though the missing test waves cannot be restored.</p><a href="#explorer" data-jump="exposure">Compare exposure definitions ↑</a></details>
    </div></section>
